+# Veterinarian Search Stays a Non-Goal; Filtering the Directory by Specialty Does Not
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declined search for pets, visits, and veterinarians in one line: only owners are searchable. The line was derived from absence during the 2026-07-31 bootstrap survey and then given the demonstration framing as its reason.
+
+A request arrived to narrow the veterinarian directory to one specialty. The directory already lists every veterinarian's specialties on the page, so the request reuses data the reader can already see. NG-9 as written covers it, because "searching for a veterinarian" reads wide enough to include any lookup on the directory. The product owner had to say which side of the line this falls on before a requirement could be written.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Keep NG-9 whole and decline the request** — the directory stays a flat list, and NG-9 needs no edit.
+2. **Withdraw NG-9** — veterinarians become searchable on the same terms as owners, including by name.
+3. **Narrow NG-9** — free-text veterinarian search stays declined; selecting one of the values the directory already displays is in scope.
+
+Option 1 declines a capability whose cost is one predicate over a collection already loaded for the page. Option 2 buys a second free-text search that repeats what owner search demonstrates, which is the pattern NG-9 exists to avoid.
+
+## Decision
+
+NG-9 is narrowed, not withdrawn (option 3). Free-text search for a veterinarian stays out of scope. Filtering the veterinarian directory by an attribute the directory already displays is in scope, and the specialty filter recorded as REQ-VET-004 is the first such filter.
+
+The line the narrowing draws: a filter selects among values the system already publishes and holds as a bounded reference list. A search takes free text from the reader and matches it against a name. Pets and visits remain unsearchable and unfilterable; the narrowing applies to the veterinarian directory alone.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- REQ-VET-004 can be written without contradicting the Non-Goals table.
+- Specialty is a bounded reference list the clinic maintains outside the application (NG-2, NG-3). A filter over it cannot grow into free-text search without another decision.
+- The demonstration framing gains a second read pattern: owner search shows paged prefix matching on typed text, and the specialty filter shows exact selection from a reference list. The two are distinct patterns, so the sample teaches something new rather than repeating itself.
+- The boundary now needs judgment at each request. "Filter by an attribute the directory already displays" is narrower than "search", and a future request will test where the two meet.
+- The rows for pets and visits in NG-9 stay wide. A request to filter either one reopens this decision.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9 — narrowed 2026-08-04. See [`REQ-VET-004`](../prd.md#req-vet-004) for the requirement the narrowing permits.
+
+## References
+
+- [prd.md § Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed NG-9 row
+- [prd.md § Veterinarian directory](../prd.md#veterinarian-directory) — REQ-VET-001, REQ-VET-003, and REQ-VET-004
